ed at the position in  england up to 2023 that's the only  uh the time frame in which the uk  government have made a commitment in  england  the commitment i've made for the next  parliament takes us up to 2026 so it's  apples and pears to make the comparison  that you have made but the other point  and this is a key point  is that in a sense the commitment i have  made is a backstop  whatever the budgetary position that the  uk government  delivers for scotland we will deliver  that transformational 20  increase in health funding but if the uk  government exceeds that and that is  extra money comes through the barnet  formula  we will commit that to the nhs as well  so by definition  we cannot be spending less than the uk  government does  in england because the commitment we've  made is the back stop  and if the barnet formula allows us to  go further we will go further but you  know we  exceeded our commitment at the last  election and we will deliver this  commitment  at least over the next parliament a 20  increase in nhs funding which amounts to  2.5  billion pounds extra money for social  care over and above that which the ifs  analysis also doesn't take into account  and  also shifting the balance of investment  so much more is going into community  health care  and mental health care too okay um  another area  i'm keen to talk about as well is drugs  scotland suffers more drug deaths per  capita than any other  european country over 1200 people dying  of drug you've misused last year  that's a record high for the sixth year  in a row how is that acceptable it's not  um and i've been very candid about that  we  have tried to tackle the drugs deaths  but we can't look at the figures for the  numbers of people  dying and conclude that what we have  done has been  enough or has been effective enough so  over the past few months we've done a  number of things  to make a concerted attempt to turn this  around so firstly  i have appointed a dedicated minister  with  responsibility for this reporting  directly  to me we have set aside a budget over  the next few years  of 250 million pounds to specifically  support initiatives  to tackle the drugs crisis and drugs  deaths and that is supporting more  community work it is supporting a  greater  provision of rehabilitation beds  including residential rehabilitation and  after-care support faster access to  treatment so that if somebody comes  forward looking for treatment they get  that quickly and aren't  having to wait for that so there is a  whole package  of policies and interventions now that  we are taking forward to turn it around  because to be frank it's not  acceptable and you know you can make  choices as a politician you can be  confronted with something like that and  try to dig your heels in and defend  the indefensible or you can do what i  think is better and  admit that this is an area where we have  not done well enough and we're  determined to do better in future  you see it sounds very good what you're  promising it sounds very candid it  sounds like  you know you're very keen to point out  what you want to change in the future  but people will be wondering where has  this been over the last 14 years that  the snp has been in power  when there's been a 160 rise in drug  deaths  and a 55 cut in real terms funding for  alcohol and drug services  is it too late look we didn't get it  right it's not because we didn't care  it's not because we weren't trying  to do things but you know we have  concluded  because i don't think we can do anything  else that we haven't got it right now i  could sit here and i could  try to set out some of the reasons why  scotland may have a higher  number of drugs deaths in other  countries reasons that go back  many decades in some respects are having  your higher drug use in the days when i  was growing up an older  drug using population with multiple  morbidities  i could see all of that and some of that  would you know be legitimate and and be  part of the explanation but i'm choosing  instead to say  we haven't done well enough here we  haven't yet  uh implemented the the policies that  have turned us around so we're going to  redouble our efforts we're going to try  different things now  i could also sit here and say that  aspects of the  the levers over this are reserved so the  classification of  drugs whether or not we can have safe  consumption rooms which is something  most experts in scotland want to see the  the levers of control of these things  still lie with westminster we don't have  all of the levers in our own hands now i  could say all of that  and all of that would have some  legitimacy but i'm actually choosing  instead to take  responsibility for not having done well  enough and for setting out  in great detail what we're going to do  to put it right i hope in future  i've been keen you know in the interview  to look back at your records  in government because it really matters  because you're effectively asking the  scottish people to trust you  to lead an independent scotland so it  really  matters you're clearly an exceptional  communicator you're clearly somebody who  has strong approval ratings relative to  other politicians  but if you look at the facts record drug  deaths after 14 years of an s p  government  still inequalities they're in education  although you admit  that in some areas there's been  improvement there are still big  inequalities when it comes to education  the highest rate of care home resident  deaths from covered  in the uk why should people trust you to  lead an independent scotland  sorry sophie the number of care home  deaths in scotland is  far too high and it shouldn't be a  statistical competition  but i i really do have to challenge that  the the number of excess deaths in  k homes in england has been much higher  than in  in scotland or higher than in scotland  in scotland we have  recorded more of them as as covert  deaths so  i will challenge that but that is not  saying that it is not the case that the  number of covet  deaths and care homes the number of  covered deaths overall hasn't been too  high because it has been  like people will decide whether they  trust me or not that's what elections  are all about i'll put my record in  front of the people of scotland i'll  point to all of the things  we have done over the past term of  parliament we have  established a new social security system  in scotland we've established  a scottish child payment which is  lifting children out of poverty  at the level of child poverty too high  but lower in scotland than in any other  part of  the uk we have made progress in closing  the attainment gap we've led the country  through the covered pandemic i'll also  be candid  with people because i think it's better  to do that than the opposite  about where i think we haven't got  things right or where we have  more work to do and then people will  make a judgment about who they  trust most to lead the country through  covet  to get us into recovery and we put  before the scottish people a bold  ambitious  transformative program for recovery from  covet  and then after covered when the time is  right to decide  the future of scotland whether we should  leave big decisions in the hands of  politicians like boris johnson or  whether we should take those decisions  into our own hands as an independent  country so that we can  shape the long-term future of scotland  in the way we want it to be and people  will make their judgments about that  they'll look at me my record what i  offer people  and they'll look at the opposition and  the competition as well and they'll  cast their verdict on may the 6th that's  democracy and i embrace it thoroughly  um alex salmond has launched a new party  to help build a super majority for  scottish independence you must be very  grateful for his help  look he can speak for himself we need a  simple majority  in the scottish parliament to give us  the mandate for an independence  referendum and then of course we need a  majority of the scottish people to win  independence and you know when i listen  to people who talk about super  majorities they sound as if they think  we can just build those our way to  independence which is almost  contemptuous  uh towards those that we need to  persuade  the many many people i  i listen to the the rhetoric around  independence and it seems to skip  over the need to do the hard work of  persuading those who voted no in 2014  uh many of whom are now open-minded i  know many of these people but want to be  persuaded and want to be treated with  respect and want  us to make a convincing persuasive case  to them and  that's the hard work that i've been  doing polling for independence is at a  higher level now than it's ever been  but we've got to continue with that hard  work with that respectful discourse and  that's how we win independence and  you know i'm not particularly interested  in an arm wrestling contest with other  people about who supports independence  the most  that's a conversation with ourselves i'm  interested in persuading those who are  open-minded but need still to be  persuaded so that we build a majority  for independence that then expresses  itself in a legitimate process  because that's how we win independence  and that's what i want to do  in the spirit of respectful discourse if  alba does win seats at the election  would you  work with alex salmond no i'm not going  to work with alex salmond  firstly i have concerns about his  personal conduct that he hasn't  acknowledged or  apologized for as i've just said i i  don't  agree with the approach to independence  because i think it is  more or is risking putting people off  rather than bringing people towards us  there's no shortcut to independence  we've got to do it through  winning a majority and allowing that  majority to express itself in a  legitimate process i also  don't know what alba stands for for me  independence is not an end in itself  it's  a means to a better scotland a fair uh  equal scotland where we value everybody  who lives here  for who they are and i don't know what  albus stands for but  from what i've seen so far i i probably  would have some concerns about that  okay um just finally we talked a lot  about lobbying  on the program this morning the snp  minister fergus ewing had a dinner  with lex grunsel and sanjeev gupta in  2017. this is according to the sunday  mail  no officials were there no notes were  taken  if there's no record of what happened  how do we know it's above board  well it was recorded in the usual way in  the the register  of meetings look i understand the  temptation  to draw an analogy between the the  scandal that we see developing around  david cameron  around the uk government and the  scottish government is dealing with gfg  the two things are very different we  work directly with gfg  to firstly uh save the dl steel works in  2015 there was virtually nobody employed  there today  employs more than a hundred people and  secondly with that company to save the  local  aluminium smelter and you know there's  more than 100 jobs have been protected  there with more created as a result the  guarantee that was agreed  to support that takeover of the smelter  the  committee of the scottish parliament  approved that so there it's not the the  lobbying  scandal that we see elsewhere and i  think people should be careful in trying  to draw  that analogy with all the proper due  diligence has been done  things like the the way in which green  cell has tried to to use the nhs  which david cameron allegedly lobbied  for that's not happened  in scotland and we've not had you know  some of the stuff around  covered loans so i can see the  temptation but the two things are not  the same  okay nicolas sturgeon thank you very  much for being on the program today  thank you  quick reminder before we go do check out  the sofie ridge on sunday podcast we're  talking about that interview with  nicolas sturgeon  it will be available from lunchtime on  the sky news app or wherever you  usually listen to your podcast after  this break it's sophie bridge the take  where we can go over everything we just  heard from my interviews  don't go anywhere  [Music]  you
 

